---
title: Custom Durability Adapter
id: custom-adapter
description: "Back resumable streams with your own store (Redis, Postgres, a queue) by implementing the five-method StreamDurability contract."
keywords:
  - custom durability adapter
  - StreamDurability
  - resumable streams
  - redis durable stream
  - postgres durable stream
  - delivery durability
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You have a store you want streams to survive on: Redis, Postgres, a queue,\nElectric, an object store. By the end of this page you have a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StreamDurability"},{"type":"text","value":"\nadapter that plugs into "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toServerSentEventsResponse"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toHttpResponse"},{"type":"text","value":", so a\nclient can reconnect to an in-flight run without re-running the model."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On Upstash Redis there is a ready-made third-party adapter: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"upstashStream()"},{"type":"text","value":" from\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@upstash/agentkit-tanstack-ai"},{"type":"text","value":". See "},{"type":"link","href":"../community-adapters/upstash#resumable-streams","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Upstash"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Core never understands your store. It only round-trips opaque offset strings you\nhand it. You implement five methods:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Method"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Job"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resumeFrom()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Return the resume offset from this request, or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" for a fresh run."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"append(chunks)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Persist a batch before delivery; return one offset per chunk, in order."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"read(offset, signal)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Replay chunks strictly after "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"offset"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"close()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mark the run complete and wake any parked readers."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"snapshot()"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Return everything stored for this run right now, without waiting."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-rules-that-matter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The rules that matter"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Get these wrong and resume breaks in subtle ways:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"loose":true,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Offsets are opaque, unique, and round-trip-safe."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Return a distinct offset\nper chunk. It travels on an SSE "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id:"},{"type":"text","value":" line or inside an NDJSON "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ id, chunk }"},{"type":"text","value":"\nenvelope, so it must survive that: core rejects an empty offset, one\ncontaining "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NUL"},{"type":"text","value":"/CR/LF, one with leading or trailing whitespace, or a\nduplicate."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"read"},{"type":"text","value":" replays strictly "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"after"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the offset"}]},{"type":"text","value":", oldest first, and ends when the\nlog is "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"closed"}]},{"type":"text","value":" — never when it sees a terminal chunk. This is the rule most\nlikely to be \"simplified\" back, so here is the reason, quoted from the\ninvariant "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"memoryStream"},{"type":"text","value":"'s own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"read"},{"type":"text","value":" loop carries in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/ai/src/stream-durability.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" (a test pins it):"}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A terminal chunk ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_FINISHED"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_ERROR"},{"type":"text","value":") does NOT end the read: an\nagent-loop run emits one per iteration ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"finishReason"},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"tool_calls\""},{"type":"text","value":" then\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"\"stop\""},{"type":"text","value":"), so stopping on the first would truncate a tool-calling run at its\nfirst tool call. The producer signals true completion by calling "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"close()"},{"type":"text","value":"\n(it does so on every exit — see "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StreamDurability.close"},{"type":"text","value":"), which sets\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"log.complete"},{"type":"text","value":". Read tails until then, or until the caller aborts."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"So an adapter that returns at the first terminal chunk truncates "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"every"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nresumed tool-calling run — the resumed client sees the first tool call and\nthen a clean end, which it reads as \"the run is over\". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"close()"},{"type":"text","value":" is your only\nend-of-log signal, and core awaits it on every producer exit (completion,\ncancellation, and failure), so tailing until then always terminates."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"read"},{"type":"text","value":" must never end the response empty while the run is still producing."}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nPark (wait for the next append) instead. A clean end with no new data tells\nthe client the run is over; if it isn't, the client fails with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DurableStreamIncompleteError"},{"type":"text","value":". Honor the abort "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"signal"},{"type":"text","value":" so a gone client\nstops the wait."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You do not handle ordering or append-before-deliver. Core buffers, calls\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"append"},{"type":"text","value":", and only forwards a chunk once you return its offset."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"snapshot"},{"type":"text","value":" never waits."}]},{"type":"text","value":" It resolves with whatever is stored right now, in\nappend order, even while the run is still producing, and resolves to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[]"},{"type":"text","value":"\nfor a run with nothing stored. Unlike "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"read"},{"type":"text","value":", it does not park: a caller\nwants to see a previous host's prefix before resuming, not tail the log. Get\nthis one wrong and resume hangs on exactly the logs that need it, because a\nproducer that crashed never called "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"close()"},{"type":"text","value":", so its log stays open forever\nand a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"read"},{"type":"text","value":" over it never finishes. In particular, do not reuse the\nunknown-run failure path a from-start "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"read"},{"type":"text","value":" join takes: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"read('-1')"},{"type":"text","value":" on an\nempty log may fail, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"snapshot()"},{"type":"text","value":" must resolve to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[]"},{"type":"text","value":". Rejecting on a\ntransport, protocol, or authorization failure is still correct."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"implement-it","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Implement it"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Write the adapter against your store's operations. Here it is over an\nappend-only per-run log you provide; swap "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunLog"},{"type":"text","value":" for your backend:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import type { StreamChunk, StreamDurability } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\n// Your backend, one append-only log per run. Back it with Redis Streams, a\n// Postgres table, a queue. Anything that returns a stable cursor per entry.\ninterface RunLog {\n  append: (chunks: Array<StreamChunk>) => Promise<Array<string>>\n  readAfter: (\n    cursor: string | null,\n  ) => Promise<Array<{ cursor: string; chunk: StreamChunk }>>\n  isComplete: () => Promise<boolean>\n  waitForChange: (signal?: AbortSignal) => Promise<void>\n  markComplete: () => Promise<void>\n  // Everything stored so far, in append order. Must not wait for more.\n  readAll: () => Promise<Array<{ cursor: string; chunk: StreamChunk }>>\n}\n\nexport function customDurability(\n  request: Request,\n  openLog: (runId: string) => RunLog,\n): StreamDurability {\n  const url = new URL(request.url)\n  // The resume offset: native SSE reconnect header first, then a join's ?offset.\n  const resume =\n    request.headers.get('Last-Event-ID') ?? url.searchParams.get('offset')\n  // Your adapter owns run identity. Resolve it the way core's own\n  // `resolveResumeRunId` does, and the way `durableStream` does too: the\n  // X-Run-Id header first (a POST producer sends this), then the ?runId\n  // query (a GET join sends this). Never mint a fresh id when neither is\n  // present — a generated id addresses a log no attach request could ever\n  // name, so the run would appear to work while writing where nobody reads.\n  const runId =\n    request.headers.get('X-Run-Id') ?? url.searchParams.get('runId')\n  if (runId === null) {\n    throw new Error(\n      'a runId is required: send it as an X-Run-Id header or a ?runId query param',\n    )\n  }\n  const log = openLog(runId)\n\n  return {\n    resumeFrom: () => resume,\n    append: (chunks) => log.append(chunks),\n    close: () => log.markComplete(),\n    read: async function* (offset, signal) {\n      // '-1' / 'now' are the from-start / from-tail join sentinels.\n      let cursor: string | null = offset === '-1' ? null : offset\n      for (;;) {\n        if (signal?.aborted) return\n        const entries = await log.readAfter(cursor)\n        for (const entry of entries) {\n          cursor = entry.cursor\n          // Yield terminal chunks like any other. An agent-loop run emits a\n          // RUN_FINISHED per iteration, so returning on one would truncate a\n          // resumed tool-calling run at its first tool call.\n          yield { offset: entry.cursor, chunk: entry.chunk }\n        }\n        // The ONLY end-of-log condition: the producer called `close()`.\n        if (await log.isComplete()) return\n        // Park. Do NOT end the response here while the producer is alive.\n        await log.waitForChange(signal)\n      }\n    },\n    snapshot: async () => {\n      const entries = await log.readAll()\n      return entries.map((entry) => ({\n        offset: entry.cursor,\n        chunk: entry.chunk,\n      }))\n    },\n  }\n}","lang":"ts","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wire it up exactly like the built-in adapters:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat, chatParamsFromRequest, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n// Your modules: the adapter above, and your backend's per-run log factory.\nimport { customDurability } from './durability'\nimport { openRunLog } from './run-log'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request)\n  const stream = chat({ adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'), messages, threadId, runId })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream, {\n    durability: { adapter: customDurability(request, openRunLog) },\n  })\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For NDJSON, swap "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toServerSentEventsResponse"},{"type":"text","value":" for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toHttpResponse"},{"type":"text","value":". The adapter\nis identical; only the wire encoding changes."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"re-persisting-a-stored-range","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Re-persisting a stored range"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Some stores can write at a key the caller chose. If yours can, you may expose\nthat as an extra capability so a caller replaying a range it already streamed can\nland each chunk back on the entry it already occupies."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You do not need it to resume a run. The recommended way to restart mid-run is to\nread the stored prefix with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"snapshot()"},{"type":"text","value":", suppress it, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"append"},{"type":"text","value":" only the\nremainder, which keeps the log append-only and works on every adapter. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./advanced#resuming-a-run-without-duplicating-what-you-already-streamed","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resuming a run without duplicating what you already streamed"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The capability is a separate method, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"upsert"},{"type":"text","value":". Implement it and your adapter is\nan "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"UpsertableStreamDurability"},{"type":"text","value":"; leave it out and it is a plain\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StreamDurability"},{"type":"text","value":". Absence is the honest signal: a consumer that needs the\ncapability asks for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"UpsertableStreamDurability"},{"type":"text","value":", so a mismatch is a compile\nerror at the wiring site rather than a failure buried in a run log. Offer it\nonly if your store can write at a key the caller chose, such as a Postgres\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (cursor) DO UPDATE"},{"type":"text","value":" or a Redis "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"XADD"},{"type":"text","value":" with an explicit,\ndeduplicated ID. A store that stamps its own cursor on every write cannot, so it\njust does not expose "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"upsert"},{"type":"text","value":", which is the choice "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"durableStream"},{"type":"text","value":" makes.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"memoryStream"},{"type":"text","value":" does expose it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each entry pairs a chunk with the offset it belongs at, so nothing has to be\nlined up by position. Validate the entire batch before touching stored state, so\na rejected call never leaves part of it applied:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"reject an offset you did not mint yourself, because every offset you minted is\nresumable by definition;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"reject an offset that repeats inside one batch;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"require a not-yet-stored offset to sit after the current tail. A caller\nreplaying an overlap therefore has to replay a contiguous suffix, and cannot\nwrite into a position it skipped earlier."}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"import type { StreamChunk, UpsertableStreamDurability } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\n// Your backend, plus the one operation `upsert` needs beyond `append`: a write\n// at a cursor you supply that replaces whatever is already stored there.\ninterface UpsertableRunLog {\n  tailSeq: () => Promise<number>\n  hasOffset: (offset: string) => Promise<boolean>\n  write: (\n    entries: Array<{ chunk: StreamChunk; offset: string }>,\n  ) => Promise<Array<string>>\n}\n\n// Offsets here are `${runId}:${seq}`. Yours can be any format you can decode\n// back into a run id and a position.\nfunction decodeSeq(runId: string, offset: string, index: number): number {\n  const prefix = `${runId}:`\n  const seq = offset.startsWith(prefix)\n    ? Number(offset.slice(prefix.length))\n    : Number.NaN\n  if (!Number.isSafeInteger(seq) || seq < 1) {\n    throw new Error(\n      `entries[${index}].offset ${JSON.stringify(offset)} was not minted by this run`,\n    )\n  }\n  return seq\n}\n\n// The returned function is `async`, so every rejection reaches the caller as a\n// rejected promise rather than a synchronous throw.\nexport function makeUpsert(\n  log: UpsertableRunLog,\n  runId: string,\n): UpsertableStreamDurability['upsert'] {\n  return async (entries) => {\n    let tail = await log.tailSeq()\n    const seen = new Set<string>()\n    for (const [index, entry] of entries.entries()) {\n      const seq = decodeSeq(runId, entry.offset, index)\n      if (seen.has(entry.offset)) {\n        throw new Error(\n          `entries[${index}].offset ${JSON.stringify(entry.offset)} is repeated in this batch`,\n        )\n      }\n      seen.add(entry.offset)\n      if (await log.hasOffset(entry.offset)) continue\n      if (seq <= tail) {\n        throw new Error(\n          `entries[${index}].offset ${JSON.stringify(entry.offset)} is not stored yet but claims position ${seq}, at or before the tail ${tail}`,\n        )\n      }\n      tail = seq\n    }\n    // Every entry passed, so the write below cannot reject partway and leave a\n    // prefix of the batch applied.\n    return log.write(entries)\n  }\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hand that to the adapter as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"upsert: makeUpsert(log, runId)"},{"type":"text","value":" alongside the five\nmethods above, and annotate the result "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"UpsertableStreamDurability"},{"type":"text","value":" so the extra\ncapability shows up in the type."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"type-your-offsets-optional","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type your offsets (optional)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StreamDurability<TOffset>"},{"type":"text","value":" is generic over the offset string. Brand it so a\nraw string can't be passed where one of your offsets is expected:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import type { StreamDurability } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\ntype MyOffset = string & { readonly __brand: 'MyOffset' }\n\n// Your adapter is then StreamDurability<MyOffset>; append/read/resumeFrom all\n// speak MyOffset, and a plain string won't type-check where one is expected.\ntype MyAdapter = StreamDurability<MyOffset>","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Core still treats the value as opaque; the brand only tightens your own code."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"terminalization-is-on-you","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Terminalization is on you"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Core awaits "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"close()"},{"type":"text","value":" on every producer exit (normal completion, cancellation,\nand failure) and appends a terminal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_ERROR"},{"type":"text","value":" on cancel/failure before\nclosing. Your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"close()"},{"type":"text","value":" must make "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"read"},{"type":"text","value":"'s "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isComplete()"},{"type":"text","value":" return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" and wake\nparked readers, so a caught-up reader stops rather than hanging — it is the only\nthing that ends a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"read"},{"type":"text","value":", since the terminal chunk it appended does not (see\n"},{"type":"link","href":"#the-rules-that-matter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the rules"}]},{"type":"text","value":"). If your backend\nproducer can die without running "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"close()"},{"type":"text","value":" (process crash), add a lease/reaper\nthat terminalizes abandoned logs. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./advanced#process-death","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Process death"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"the-rules-that-matter","text":"The rules that matter","level":2},{"id":"implement-it","text":"Implement it","level":2},{"id":"re-persisting-a-stored-range","text":"Re-persisting a stored range","level":2},{"id":"type-your-offsets-optional","text":"Type your offsets (optional)","level":2},{"id":"terminalization-is-on-you","text":"Terminalization is on you","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nYou have a store you want streams to survive on: Redis, Postgres, a queue,\nElectric, an object store. By the end of this page you have a `StreamDurability`\nadapter that plugs into `toServerSentEventsResponse` / `toHttpResponse`, so a\nclient can reconnect to an in-flight run without re-running the model.\n\n> On Upstash Redis there is a ready-made third-party adapter: `upstashStream()` from\n> `@upstash/agentkit-tanstack-ai`. See [Upstash](../community-adapters/upstash#resumable-streams).\n\nCore never understands your store. It only round-trips opaque offset strings you\nhand it. You implement five methods:\n\n| Method | Job |\n| --- | --- |\n| `resumeFrom()` | Return the resume offset from this request, or `null` for a fresh run. |\n| `append(chunks)` | Persist a batch before delivery; return one offset per chunk, in order. |\n| `read(offset, signal)` | Replay chunks strictly after `offset`. |\n| `close()` | Mark the run complete and wake any parked readers. |\n| `snapshot()` | Return everything stored for this run right now, without waiting. |\n\n## The rules that matter\n\nGet these wrong and resume breaks in subtle ways:\n\n- **Offsets are opaque, unique, and round-trip-safe.** Return a distinct offset\n  per chunk. It travels on an SSE `id:` line or inside an NDJSON `{ id, chunk }`\n  envelope, so it must survive that: core rejects an empty offset, one\n  containing `NUL`/CR/LF, one with leading or trailing whitespace, or a\n  duplicate.\n- **`read` replays strictly *after* the offset**, oldest first, and ends when the\n  log is **closed** — never when it sees a terminal chunk. This is the rule most\n  likely to be \"simplified\" back, so here is the reason, quoted from the\n  invariant `memoryStream`'s own `read` loop carries in\n  `packages/ai/src/stream-durability.ts` (a test pins it):\n\n  > A terminal chunk (`RUN_FINISHED` / `RUN_ERROR`) does NOT end the read: an\n  > agent-loop run emits one per iteration (`finishReason` `\"tool_calls\"` then\n  > `\"stop\"`), so stopping on the first would truncate a tool-calling run at its\n  > first tool call. The producer signals true completion by calling `close()`\n  > (it does so on every exit — see `StreamDurability.close`), which sets\n  > `log.complete`. Read tails until then, or until the caller aborts.\n\n  So an adapter that returns at the first terminal chunk truncates **every**\n  resumed tool-calling run — the resumed client sees the first tool call and\n  then a clean end, which it reads as \"the run is over\". `close()` is your only\n  end-of-log signal, and core awaits it on every producer exit (completion,\n  cancellation, and failure), so tailing until then always terminates.\n- **`read` must never end the response empty while the run is still producing.**\n  Park (wait for the next append) instead. A clean end with no new data tells\n  the client the run is over; if it isn't, the client fails with\n  `DurableStreamIncompleteError`. Honor the abort `signal` so a gone client\n  stops the wait.\n- You do not handle ordering or append-before-deliver. Core buffers, calls\n  `append`, and only forwards a chunk once you return its offset.\n- **`snapshot` never waits.** It resolves with whatever is stored right now, in\n  append order, even while the run is still producing, and resolves to `[]`\n  for a run with nothing stored. Unlike `read`, it does not park: a caller\n  wants to see a previous host's prefix before resuming, not tail the log. Get\n  this one wrong and resume hangs on exactly the logs that need it, because a\n  producer that crashed never called `close()`, so its log stays open forever\n  and a `read` over it never finishes. In particular, do not reuse the\n  unknown-run failure path a from-start `read` join takes: `read('-1')` on an\n  empty log may fail, `snapshot()` must resolve to `[]`. Rejecting on a\n  transport, protocol, or authorization failure is still correct.\n\n## Implement it\n\nWrite the adapter against your store's operations. Here it is over an\nappend-only per-run log you provide; swap `RunLog` for your backend:\n\n```ts ignore\nimport type { StreamChunk, StreamDurability } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\n// Your backend, one append-only log per run. Back it with Redis Streams, a\n// Postgres table, a queue. Anything that returns a stable cursor per entry.\ninterface RunLog {\n  append: (chunks: Array<StreamChunk>) => Promise<Array<string>>\n  readAfter: (\n    cursor: string | null,\n  ) => Promise<Array<{ cursor: string; chunk: StreamChunk }>>\n  isComplete: () => Promise<boolean>\n  waitForChange: (signal?: AbortSignal) => Promise<void>\n  markComplete: () => Promise<void>\n  // Everything stored so far, in append order. Must not wait for more.\n  readAll: () => Promise<Array<{ cursor: string; chunk: StreamChunk }>>\n}\n\nexport function customDurability(\n  request: Request,\n  openLog: (runId: string) => RunLog,\n): StreamDurability {\n  const url = new URL(request.url)\n  // The resume offset: native SSE reconnect header first, then a join's ?offset.\n  const resume =\n    request.headers.get('Last-Event-ID') ?? url.searchParams.get('offset')\n  // Your adapter owns run identity. Resolve it the way core's own\n  // `resolveResumeRunId` does, and the way `durableStream` does too: the\n  // X-Run-Id header first (a POST producer sends this), then the ?runId\n  // query (a GET join sends this). Never mint a fresh id when neither is\n  // present — a generated id addresses a log no attach request could ever\n  // name, so the run would appear to work while writing where nobody reads.\n  const runId =\n    request.headers.get('X-Run-Id') ?? url.searchParams.get('runId')\n  if (runId === null) {\n    throw new Error(\n      'a runId is required: send it as an X-Run-Id header or a ?runId query param',\n    )\n  }\n  const log = openLog(runId)\n\n  return {\n    resumeFrom: () => resume,\n    append: (chunks) => log.append(chunks),\n    close: () => log.markComplete(),\n    read: async function* (offset, signal) {\n      // '-1' / 'now' are the from-start / from-tail join sentinels.\n      let cursor: string | null = offset === '-1' ? null : offset\n      for (;;) {\n        if (signal?.aborted) return\n        const entries = await log.readAfter(cursor)\n        for (const entry of entries) {\n          cursor = entry.cursor\n          // Yield terminal chunks like any other. An agent-loop run emits a\n          // RUN_FINISHED per iteration, so returning on one would truncate a\n          // resumed tool-calling run at its first tool call.\n          yield { offset: entry.cursor, chunk: entry.chunk }\n        }\n        // The ONLY end-of-log condition: the producer called `close()`.\n        if (await log.isComplete()) return\n        // Park. Do NOT end the response here while the producer is alive.\n        await log.waitForChange(signal)\n      }\n    },\n    snapshot: async () => {\n      const entries = await log.readAll()\n      return entries.map((entry) => ({\n        offset: entry.cursor,\n        chunk: entry.chunk,\n      }))\n    },\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWire it up exactly like the built-in adapters:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, chatParamsFromRequest, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n// Your modules: the adapter above, and your backend's per-run log factory.\nimport { customDurability } from './durability'\nimport { openRunLog } from './run-log'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request)\n  const stream = chat({ adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'), messages, threadId, runId })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream, {\n    durability: { adapter: customDurability(request, openRunLog) },\n  })\n}\n```\n\nFor NDJSON, swap `toServerSentEventsResponse` for `toHttpResponse`. The adapter\nis identical; only the wire encoding changes.\n\n## Re-persisting a stored range\n\nSome stores can write at a key the caller chose. If yours can, you may expose\nthat as an extra capability so a caller replaying a range it already streamed can\nland each chunk back on the entry it already occupies.\n\nYou do not need it to resume a run. The recommended way to restart mid-run is to\nread the stored prefix with `snapshot()`, suppress it, and `append` only the\nremainder, which keeps the log append-only and works on every adapter. See\n[Resuming a run without duplicating what you already streamed](./advanced#resuming-a-run-without-duplicating-what-you-already-streamed).\n\nThe capability is a separate method, `upsert`. Implement it and your adapter is\nan `UpsertableStreamDurability`; leave it out and it is a plain\n`StreamDurability`. Absence is the honest signal: a consumer that needs the\ncapability asks for `UpsertableStreamDurability`, so a mismatch is a compile\nerror at the wiring site rather than a failure buried in a run log. Offer it\nonly if your store can write at a key the caller chose, such as a Postgres\n`INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (cursor) DO UPDATE` or a Redis `XADD` with an explicit,\ndeduplicated ID. A store that stamps its own cursor on every write cannot, so it\njust does not expose `upsert`, which is the choice `durableStream` makes.\n`memoryStream` does expose it.\n\nEach entry pairs a chunk with the offset it belongs at, so nothing has to be\nlined up by position. Validate the entire batch before touching stored state, so\na rejected call never leaves part of it applied:\n\n- reject an offset you did not mint yourself, because every offset you minted is\n  resumable by definition;\n- reject an offset that repeats inside one batch;\n- require a not-yet-stored offset to sit after the current tail. A caller\n  replaying an overlap therefore has to replay a contiguous suffix, and cannot\n  write into a position it skipped earlier.\n\n```ts\nimport type { StreamChunk, UpsertableStreamDurability } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\n// Your backend, plus the one operation `upsert` needs beyond `append`: a write\n// at a cursor you supply that replaces whatever is already stored there.\ninterface UpsertableRunLog {\n  tailSeq: () => Promise<number>\n  hasOffset: (offset: string) => Promise<boolean>\n  write: (\n    entries: Array<{ chunk: StreamChunk; offset: string }>,\n  ) => Promise<Array<string>>\n}\n\n// Offsets here are `${runId}:${seq}`. Yours can be any format you can decode\n// back into a run id and a position.\nfunction decodeSeq(runId: string, offset: string, index: number): number {\n  const prefix = `${runId}:`\n  const seq = offset.startsWith(prefix)\n    ? Number(offset.slice(prefix.length))\n    : Number.NaN\n  if (!Number.isSafeInteger(seq) || seq < 1) {\n    throw new Error(\n      `entries[${index}].offset ${JSON.stringify(offset)} was not minted by this run`,\n    )\n  }\n  return seq\n}\n\n// The returned function is `async`, so every rejection reaches the caller as a\n// rejected promise rather than a synchronous throw.\nexport function makeUpsert(\n  log: UpsertableRunLog,\n  runId: string,\n): UpsertableStreamDurability['upsert'] {\n  return async (entries) => {\n    let tail = await log.tailSeq()\n    const seen = new Set<string>()\n    for (const [index, entry] of entries.entries()) {\n      const seq = decodeSeq(runId, entry.offset, index)\n      if (seen.has(entry.offset)) {\n        throw new Error(\n          `entries[${index}].offset ${JSON.stringify(entry.offset)} is repeated in this batch`,\n        )\n      }\n      seen.add(entry.offset)\n      if (await log.hasOffset(entry.offset)) continue\n      if (seq <= tail) {\n        throw new Error(\n          `entries[${index}].offset ${JSON.stringify(entry.offset)} is not stored yet but claims position ${seq}, at or before the tail ${tail}`,\n        )\n      }\n      tail = seq\n    }\n    // Every entry passed, so the write below cannot reject partway and leave a\n    // prefix of the batch applied.\n    return log.write(entries)\n  }\n}\n```\n\nHand that to the adapter as `upsert: makeUpsert(log, runId)` alongside the five\nmethods above, and annotate the result `UpsertableStreamDurability` so the extra\ncapability shows up in the type.\n\n## Type your offsets (optional)\n\n`StreamDurability<TOffset>` is generic over the offset string. Brand it so a\nraw string can't be passed where one of your offsets is expected:\n\n```ts\nimport type { StreamDurability } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\ntype MyOffset = string & { readonly __brand: 'MyOffset' }\n\n// Your adapter is then StreamDurability<MyOffset>; append/read/resumeFrom all\n// speak MyOffset, and a plain string won't type-check where one is expected.\ntype MyAdapter = StreamDurability<MyOffset>\n```\n\nCore still treats the value as opaque; the brand only tightens your own code.\n\n## Terminalization is on you\n\nCore awaits `close()` on every producer exit (normal completion, cancellation,\nand failure) and appends a terminal `RUN_ERROR` on cancel/failure before\nclosing. Your `close()` must make `read`'s `isComplete()` return `true` and wake\nparked readers, so a caught-up reader stops rather than hanging — it is the only\nthing that ends a `read`, since the terminal chunk it appended does not (see\n[the rules](#the-rules-that-matter)). If your backend\nproducer can die without running `close()` (process crash), add a lease/reaper\nthat terminalizes abandoned logs. See [Process death](./advanced#process-death).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Custom Durability Adapter\nid: custom-adapter\ndescription: \"Back resumable streams with your own store (Redis, Postgres, a queue) by implementing the five-method StreamDurability contract.\"\nkeywords:\n  - custom durability adapter\n  - StreamDurability\n  - resumable streams\n  - redis durable stream\n  - postgres durable stream\n  - delivery durability\n---\n\nYou have a store you want streams to survive on: Redis, Postgres, a queue,\nElectric, an object store. By the end of this page you have a `StreamDurability`\nadapter that plugs into `toServerSentEventsResponse` / `toHttpResponse`, so a\nclient can reconnect to an in-flight run without re-running the model.\n\n> On Upstash Redis there is a ready-made third-party adapter: `upstashStream()` from\n> `@upstash/agentkit-tanstack-ai`. See [Upstash](../community-adapters/upstash#resumable-streams).\n\nCore never understands your store. It only round-trips opaque offset strings you\nhand it. You implement five methods:\n\n| Method | Job |\n| --- | --- |\n| `resumeFrom()` | Return the resume offset from this request, or `null` for a fresh run. |\n| `append(chunks)` | Persist a batch before delivery; return one offset per chunk, in order. |\n| `read(offset, signal)` | Replay chunks strictly after `offset`. |\n| `close()` | Mark the run complete and wake any parked readers. |\n| `snapshot()` | Return everything stored for this run right now, without waiting. |\n\n## The rules that matter\n\nGet these wrong and resume breaks in subtle ways:\n\n- **Offsets are opaque, unique, and round-trip-safe.** Return a distinct offset\n  per chunk. It travels on an SSE `id:` line or inside an NDJSON `{ id, chunk }`\n  envelope, so it must survive that: core rejects an empty offset, one\n  containing `NUL`/CR/LF, one with leading or trailing whitespace, or a\n  duplicate.\n- **`read` replays strictly *after* the offset**, oldest first, and ends when the\n  log is **closed** — never when it sees a terminal chunk. This is the rule most\n  likely to be \"simplified\" back, so here is the reason, quoted from the\n  invariant `memoryStream`'s own `read` loop carries in\n  `packages/ai/src/stream-durability.ts` (a test pins it):\n\n  > A terminal chunk (`RUN_FINISHED` / `RUN_ERROR`) does NOT end the read: an\n  > agent-loop run emits one per iteration (`finishReason` `\"tool_calls\"` then\n  > `\"stop\"`), so stopping on the first would truncate a tool-calling run at its\n  > first tool call. The producer signals true completion by calling `close()`\n  > (it does so on every exit — see `StreamDurability.close`), which sets\n  > `log.complete`. Read tails until then, or until the caller aborts.\n\n  So an adapter that returns at the first terminal chunk truncates **every**\n  resumed tool-calling run — the resumed client sees the first tool call and\n  then a clean end, which it reads as \"the run is over\". `close()` is your only\n  end-of-log signal, and core awaits it on every producer exit (completion,\n  cancellation, and failure), so tailing until then always terminates.\n- **`read` must never end the response empty while the run is still producing.**\n  Park (wait for the next append) instead. A clean end with no new data tells\n  the client the run is over; if it isn't, the client fails with\n  `DurableStreamIncompleteError`. Honor the abort `signal` so a gone client\n  stops the wait.\n- You do not handle ordering or append-before-deliver. Core buffers, calls\n  `append`, and only forwards a chunk once you return its offset.\n- **`snapshot` never waits.** It resolves with whatever is stored right now, in\n  append order, even while the run is still producing, and resolves to `[]`\n  for a run with nothing stored. Unlike `read`, it does not park: a caller\n  wants to see a previous host's prefix before resuming, not tail the log. Get\n  this one wrong and resume hangs on exactly the logs that need it, because a\n  producer that crashed never called `close()`, so its log stays open forever\n  and a `read` over it never finishes. In particular, do not reuse the\n  unknown-run failure path a from-start `read` join takes: `read('-1')` on an\n  empty log may fail, `snapshot()` must resolve to `[]`. Rejecting on a\n  transport, protocol, or authorization failure is still correct.\n\n## Implement it\n\nWrite the adapter against your store's operations. Here it is over an\nappend-only per-run log you provide; swap `RunLog` for your backend:\n\n```ts ignore\nimport type { StreamChunk, StreamDurability } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\n// Your backend, one append-only log per run. Back it with Redis Streams, a\n// Postgres table, a queue. Anything that returns a stable cursor per entry.\ninterface RunLog {\n  append: (chunks: Array<StreamChunk>) => Promise<Array<string>>\n  readAfter: (\n    cursor: string | null,\n  ) => Promise<Array<{ cursor: string; chunk: StreamChunk }>>\n  isComplete: () => Promise<boolean>\n  waitForChange: (signal?: AbortSignal) => Promise<void>\n  markComplete: () => Promise<void>\n  // Everything stored so far, in append order. Must not wait for more.\n  readAll: () => Promise<Array<{ cursor: string; chunk: StreamChunk }>>\n}\n\nexport function customDurability(\n  request: Request,\n  openLog: (runId: string) => RunLog,\n): StreamDurability {\n  const url = new URL(request.url)\n  // The resume offset: native SSE reconnect header first, then a join's ?offset.\n  const resume =\n    request.headers.get('Last-Event-ID') ?? url.searchParams.get('offset')\n  // Your adapter owns run identity. Resolve it the way core's own\n  // `resolveResumeRunId` does, and the way `durableStream` does too: the\n  // X-Run-Id header first (a POST producer sends this), then the ?runId\n  // query (a GET join sends this). Never mint a fresh id when neither is\n  // present — a generated id addresses a log no attach request could ever\n  // name, so the run would appear to work while writing where nobody reads.\n  const runId =\n    request.headers.get('X-Run-Id') ?? url.searchParams.get('runId')\n  if (runId === null) {\n    throw new Error(\n      'a runId is required: send it as an X-Run-Id header or a ?runId query param',\n    )\n  }\n  const log = openLog(runId)\n\n  return {\n    resumeFrom: () => resume,\n    append: (chunks) => log.append(chunks),\n    close: () => log.markComplete(),\n    read: async function* (offset, signal) {\n      // '-1' / 'now' are the from-start / from-tail join sentinels.\n      let cursor: string | null = offset === '-1' ? null : offset\n      for (;;) {\n        if (signal?.aborted) return\n        const entries = await log.readAfter(cursor)\n        for (const entry of entries) {\n          cursor = entry.cursor\n          // Yield terminal chunks like any other. An agent-loop run emits a\n          // RUN_FINISHED per iteration, so returning on one would truncate a\n          // resumed tool-calling run at its first tool call.\n          yield { offset: entry.cursor, chunk: entry.chunk }\n        }\n        // The ONLY end-of-log condition: the producer called `close()`.\n        if (await log.isComplete()) return\n        // Park. Do NOT end the response here while the producer is alive.\n        await log.waitForChange(signal)\n      }\n    },\n    snapshot: async () => {\n      const entries = await log.readAll()\n      return entries.map((entry) => ({\n        offset: entry.cursor,\n        chunk: entry.chunk,\n      }))\n    },\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWire it up exactly like the built-in adapters:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, chatParamsFromRequest, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n// Your modules: the adapter above, and your backend's per-run log factory.\nimport { customDurability } from './durability'\nimport { openRunLog } from './run-log'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request)\n  const stream = chat({ adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'), messages, threadId, runId })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream, {\n    durability: { adapter: customDurability(request, openRunLog) },\n  })\n}\n```\n\nFor NDJSON, swap `toServerSentEventsResponse` for `toHttpResponse`. The adapter\nis identical; only the wire encoding changes.\n\n## Re-persisting a stored range\n\nSome stores can write at a key the caller chose. If yours can, you may expose\nthat as an extra capability so a caller replaying a range it already streamed can\nland each chunk back on the entry it already occupies.\n\nYou do not need it to resume a run. The recommended way to restart mid-run is to\nread the stored prefix with `snapshot()`, suppress it, and `append` only the\nremainder, which keeps the log append-only and works on every adapter. See\n[Resuming a run without duplicating what you already streamed](./advanced#resuming-a-run-without-duplicating-what-you-already-streamed).\n\nThe capability is a separate method, `upsert`. Implement it and your adapter is\nan `UpsertableStreamDurability`; leave it out and it is a plain\n`StreamDurability`. Absence is the honest signal: a consumer that needs the\ncapability asks for `UpsertableStreamDurability`, so a mismatch is a compile\nerror at the wiring site rather than a failure buried in a run log. Offer it\nonly if your store can write at a key the caller chose, such as a Postgres\n`INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (cursor) DO UPDATE` or a Redis `XADD` with an explicit,\ndeduplicated ID. A store that stamps its own cursor on every write cannot, so it\njust does not expose `upsert`, which is the choice `durableStream` makes.\n`memoryStream` does expose it.\n\nEach entry pairs a chunk with the offset it belongs at, so nothing has to be\nlined up by position. Validate the entire batch before touching stored state, so\na rejected call never leaves part of it applied:\n\n- reject an offset you did not mint yourself, because every offset you minted is\n  resumable by definition;\n- reject an offset that repeats inside one batch;\n- require a not-yet-stored offset to sit after the current tail. A caller\n  replaying an overlap therefore has to replay a contiguous suffix, and cannot\n  write into a position it skipped earlier.\n\n```ts\nimport type { StreamChunk, UpsertableStreamDurability } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\n// Your backend, plus the one operation `upsert` needs beyond `append`: a write\n// at a cursor you supply that replaces whatever is already stored there.\ninterface UpsertableRunLog {\n  tailSeq: () => Promise<number>\n  hasOffset: (offset: string) => Promise<boolean>\n  write: (\n    entries: Array<{ chunk: StreamChunk; offset: string }>,\n  ) => Promise<Array<string>>\n}\n\n// Offsets here are `${runId}:${seq}`. Yours can be any format you can decode\n// back into a run id and a position.\nfunction decodeSeq(runId: string, offset: string, index: number): number {\n  const prefix = `${runId}:`\n  const seq = offset.startsWith(prefix)\n    ? Number(offset.slice(prefix.length))\n    : Number.NaN\n  if (!Number.isSafeInteger(seq) || seq < 1) {\n    throw new Error(\n      `entries[${index}].offset ${JSON.stringify(offset)} was not minted by this run`,\n    )\n  }\n  return seq\n}\n\n// The returned function is `async`, so every rejection reaches the caller as a\n// rejected promise rather than a synchronous throw.\nexport function makeUpsert(\n  log: UpsertableRunLog,\n  runId: string,\n): UpsertableStreamDurability['upsert'] {\n  return async (entries) => {\n    let tail = await log.tailSeq()\n    const seen = new Set<string>()\n    for (const [index, entry] of entries.entries()) {\n      const seq = decodeSeq(runId, entry.offset, index)\n      if (seen.has(entry.offset)) {\n        throw new Error(\n          `entries[${index}].offset ${JSON.stringify(entry.offset)} is repeated in this batch`,\n        )\n      }\n      seen.add(entry.offset)\n      if (await log.hasOffset(entry.offset)) continue\n      if (seq <= tail) {\n        throw new Error(\n          `entries[${index}].offset ${JSON.stringify(entry.offset)} is not stored yet but claims position ${seq}, at or before the tail ${tail}`,\n        )\n      }\n      tail = seq\n    }\n    // Every entry passed, so the write below cannot reject partway and leave a\n    // prefix of the batch applied.\n    return log.write(entries)\n  }\n}\n```\n\nHand that to the adapter as `upsert: makeUpsert(log, runId)` alongside the five\nmethods above, and annotate the result `UpsertableStreamDurability` so the extra\ncapability shows up in the type.\n\n## Type your offsets (optional)\n\n`StreamDurability<TOffset>` is generic over the offset string. Brand it so a\nraw string can't be passed where one of your offsets is expected:\n\n```ts\nimport type { StreamDurability } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\ntype MyOffset = string & { readonly __brand: 'MyOffset' }\n\n// Your adapter is then StreamDurability<MyOffset>; append/read/resumeFrom all\n// speak MyOffset, and a plain string won't type-check where one is expected.\ntype MyAdapter = StreamDurability<MyOffset>\n```\n\nCore still treats the value as opaque; the brand only tightens your own code.\n\n## Terminalization is on you\n\nCore awaits `close()` on every producer exit (normal completion, cancellation,\nand failure) and appends a terminal `RUN_ERROR` on cancel/failure before\nclosing. Your `close()` must make `read`'s `isComplete()` return `true` and wake\nparked readers, so a caught-up reader stops rather than hanging — it is the only\nthing that ends a `read`, since the terminal chunk it appended does not (see\n[the rules](#the-rules-that-matter)). If your backend\nproducer can die without running `close()` (process crash), add a lease/reaper\nthat terminalizes abandoned logs. See [Process death](./advanced#process-death).\n"}
